theseusd install --user: the plan. Nothing is changed: --apply performs it.
  operator:  ada (uid 1000)
  binary:    /opt/theseus/bin/theseusd
  config:    op://Example/theseus-config/notesPlain
  state dir: the config's [server] state_dir (default ~/.theseus), as today
  PATH:      this shell's, so the daemon finds `op` and your jobs their tools

  ok      token  /home/ada/.config/theseus/op-token (yours alone: mode 0600 or stricter, not empty)
                 the 1Password token the daemon reads when it starts: its owner, mode, and size are checked, and it is never opened
  write   file   /home/ada/.config/systemd/user/theseusd.service ada:ada 0644
                 the daemon as your systemd user service
                 | # Written by `theseusd install --user`. A later --apply rewrites it:
                 | # put your own changes in a drop-in (`systemctl --user edit theseusd`).
                 | [Unit]
                 | Description=Theseus daemon
                 | StartLimitIntervalSec=300
                 | StartLimitBurst=10
                 |
                 | [Service]
                 | Type=exec
                 | ExecStart=/opt/theseus/bin/theseusd --config op://Example/theseus-config/notesPlain --op-token-file /home/ada/.config/theseus/op-token
                 | Environment="PATH=/home/ada/.local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin"
                 | Environment="LANG=C.UTF-8"
                 | # SIGINT is the daemon's clean stop: it removes its socket and closes the
                 | # store. A stop signals the daemon alone, so running jobs finish, and the
                 | # next daemon reads their results from the spool, as a shell-started
                 | # daemon's stop leaves them.
                 | KillSignal=SIGINT
                 | KillMode=process
                 | Restart=on-failure
                 | RestartSec=1
                 | # The unit's cgroup is the daemon's own: each L0 job gets a cgroup of its
                 | # own inside it, with a process cap and an exact stop.
                 | Delegate=yes
                 | # Open files (theseus-7vtp): systemd's soft limit of 1,024 ended the daemon at
                 | # about a thousand connections. The daemon raises its own soft limit to this
                 | # at start and holds its connections to a ceiling under it.
                 | LimitNOFILE=65536
                 | # The tasks the unit may hold: the daemon's threads and children (under 2,000),
                 | # and the admission ceiling's 8 executions at a job's cap of 4,096 each, with
                 | # room above, so a fork bomb in one job meets its own cap first.
                 | TasksMax=49152
                 |
                 | [Install]
                 | WantedBy=default.target

1 to do, 1 already done.

Then, by hand:
  - systemctl --user daemon-reload
  - stop the daemon you started by hand (`theseus shutdown`), so the unit's can take its socket
  - systemctl --user enable --now theseusd.service
  - loginctl enable-linger ada: your user manager, and the daemon, keep running when you log out
  - journalctl --user -u theseusd: its log
